{
  "summary": "Protects the IPC event loop in `CapnpProtocol` with a dedicated mutex and acquires explicit `EventLoopRef` instances across protocol operations (`connect`, `listen`, `makeStream`, and `disconnectIncoming`). It also holds a temporary reference during `serve()` initialization to ensure callbacks do not see the loop as shutting down before the parent connection is established.",
  "problem": "In multiprocess `serve()` mode, the IPC event loop relies entirely on the parent connection's reference to remain running. Methods invoked from other threads lack loop references and access `m_loop` without synchronization, leaving them vulnerable to data races or crashes if called concurrently with loop shutdown or parent disconnection.",
  "evidence": [
    "PR description: \"EventLoop::loop() exits when its reference count reaches zero. In serve() mode, the parent connection is the only reference keeping the loop running. Other CapnpProtocol methods called from threads not handling parent IPC requests do not hold their own references and therefore depend on the parent connection remaining alive.\""
  ],
